  <h2 id="two-machines-two-roles">Two machines, two roles</h2>
  <p>
    The biggest difference is that these machines were designed for different kinds of sewing.
    The <strong>Juki 8700H</strong>
    is an <strong>industrial 1-needle lockstitch machine</strong>. It is meant to sew one stitch
    type very well: a fast, reliable straight lockstitch. The <strong>Juki HZL-F600</strong> is a
    <strong>computerized home sewing machine</strong> built to do many jobs: straight stitch, zigzag,
    buttonholes, decorative stitches, quilting features, and more.
  </p>
  <p>
    That means the 8700H usually feels better when the job is repetitive, structural, and
    material-driven: long seams, medium-to-heavy assemblies, bags, denser woven fabrics, and
    prototype construction where speed and seam consistency matter. The HZL-F600 usually feels
    better when the job is varied: garment details, edge finishing, lighter fabrics, test
    pieces, quilting, and projects where changing stitch type matters.
  </p>

